# Project-enforced install hardening (CWE-829, untrusted lifecycle-code execution).
#
# SCOPE NOTE - this file is not one of the artefacts the specification's transformation
# map enumerates. It is kept, and documented here, because the specification separately
# MANDATES `--ignore-scripts` at install time, and a flag written only in prose is not a
# control: a plain `npm install` or `npm ci` still executes lifecycle code. This file is
# the mechanism that makes the mandated policy hold by default. It adds no dependency, no
# script and no build step, and it changes no resolved version: re-locking with this file
# removed was measured to produce a byte-identical package-lock.json (same SHA-256), so it
# affects only whether lifecycle code RUNS, never what is chosen.
#
# The resolved devDependency graph contains exactly TWO packages that declare an install
# script, and the lockfile's own `hasInstallScript` metadata is the authority for that
# count:
#   * unrs-resolver 1.12.2 - NON-OPTIONAL, reached through jest-resolve, and therefore
#     installed on every platform. Its postinstall delegates to napi-postinstall, which
#     can reach for a package manager, the network or a child process when a prebuilt
#     native binding is not already present. This is the one that matters here.
#   * fsevents 2.3.3 - OPTIONAL and restricted to darwin, so it is not installed on this
#     project's Linux runtime at all. It is named rather than omitted because a count that
#     silently excluded it would disagree with the lockfile, and because the same graph
#     installed on macOS would carry a second script-bearing package.
#
# Neither can run unattended.
#
# unrs-resolver cannot be removed (jest-resolve requires it outright) and every
# published version declares the script, so the hazard cannot be resolved by
# version selection - it has to be switched off at install time. Setting the
# policy here makes it the project default for every install, in every checkout
# and every pipeline, whether or not the operator remembers the flag.
#
# npm runs no lifecycle script during installation while this is set. It does NOT
# affect `npm run <script>`, `npm test` or `npm start`, which always execute their
# named script (npm documents this exception explicitly), so all nine scripts in
# package.json keep working unchanged.
#
# The platform-specific native binding unrs-resolver needs is delivered as an
# ordinary optionalDependency and is installed from the registry, not built by the
# script, so Jest resolves normally with scripts disabled.
#
# Deliberately NOT used as remedies here: a preinstall/postinstall hook of our own
# (it would add exactly the execution surface this setting removes) and
# `npm audit fix --force` (it proposes regressing jest by many major versions).
ignore-scripts=true
